<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.1.4: Explain Why an Expression Is a Perfect Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02328</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.1.4: Explain Why an Expression Is a Perfect Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d1f599c4-e0a6-4f8e-81b7-442ff8ec34f3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Explain why it makes sense to call the expression \(x^2+20x+100\) a perfect square.</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> It is equivalent to \((x+10)^2\), so it is a factor squared.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve \(x^2+20x+100=81\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(x = -1\) and \(x = -19\)</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>If students solve the second equation by factoring and using the square root property, state that some equations cannot be easily factored and that Lessons 9.2-9.4 explore equations that cannot always be easily factored but can be solved by completing the square.</para></section>
</content>
</document>
